{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-regime underprediction: when the last 5–10 days show a clear breakout (new 30d highs / accelerating slope), the model lags the rally and stays too flat. Apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small at days 1–3, large by days 10–30).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors increase with lead time. Near-term (1–3d) is only slightly low; medium/long horizon (7–30d) is materially too low. Use a ramped correction (increasing additive premium with forecast step).",
    "Over-mean-reversion after strength: the model repeatedly forecasts pullbacks back toward ~70–72 even while the realized path trends to ~78–84. Reduce mean-reversion strength (i.e., keep forecasts closer to recent trend/upper range) during momentum regimes.",
    "Trend/volatility underestimation: the model underestimates both the drift and the amplitude during the rally (misses the size of daily gains and the sustained climb). Inflate trend component (or add momentum term) and widen expected move as realized volatility rises.",
    "Spike handling: after sharp up-moves, the model tends to assume quick normalization; in these examples the market consolidates at a higher plateau instead. Replace 'post-spike fade' with 'post-spike partial retention' (only fade a fraction of the jump unless price breaks back below prior breakout level)."
  ]
}
